From: challoc <noreply@chainguard.dev>
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] memefficiency: calibrate the active defrag tests for challoc

Under MALLOC=challoc, with challoc's cache GC run from defrag hints and
epoch writes (challoc PR #576), fifteen active defrag tests in
tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl fail, each for one of two reasons. Most
assume the bundled jemalloc's slab geometry. The replicaof flush test
assumes the fragmentation jemalloc leaves right after a flush. Every
change goes through a new helper, challoc_or, which returns the challoc
value when mem_allocator contains "challoc" and the stock value
otherwise. With the bundled jemalloc the file behaves as before.

Figures are from x86_64 with challoc built for quantum 8 (the bundled
jemalloc's lg_quantum=3), `--single unit/memefficiency`, against stock
Redis 8.10.3 with its bundled jemalloc on the same host.

1. The fragmentation floor: "eval scripts", "IDMP streams", the three
   "HFE" tests and "argv retained by the main thread from IO thread"
   (each standalone and cluster), "big list" and "template-encoded
   hashes".

   challoc puts every size class up to 4 KiB in 64 KiB slabs; jemalloc's
   bin slabs are one to a few pages. Active defrag leaves a floor of
   partly used slabs that no pass recovers: about one slab per class,
   plus every slab that still holds a long-lived object the
   defragmenter does not move. Flushing every thread's cache under gdb
   once defrag has stalled lowers frag_bytes by less than 0.2 MB, so the
   floor is not cache residue.

   The floor at the end of these tests is 1.5 to 1.9 MB when each test
   runs alone on a fresh server. When the whole file runs it is 3.0 to
   4.8 MB, because objects left by earlier tests pin more slabs. The same
   points under jemalloc measure 0.5 to 2.5 MB. These tests end with 25
   to 30 MB of data outside the Lua arena, so the floor alone is a ratio
   of 1.06 to 1.19. That is above their 1.05..1.1 targets. The floor is
   also above their 100kb..2mb active-defrag-ignore-bytes, so the
   defragmenter never stops: over the 50 s wait it rescans the keyspace
   and moves nothing (active_defrag_misses grows to 0.35e9..1.7e9, with
   a few hundred hits).

   Under challoc these tests set active-defrag-ignore-bytes to 6mb. The
   final ratio is 1.25 for the tests that require 1.05..1.1; "big list"
   keeps its 1.1 (measured 1.07). The ratio before defrag is still 1.8
   to 2.1 (20 to 27 MB of fragmentation), so defrag still has to bring
   it down to the floor.

   The pre-delete checks of IDMP streams, HFE and argv require a ratio
   under 1.05 or 1.07. That is the same floor before any delete
   (measured 1.01 to 1.08), so under challoc they take 1.1.

   Unchanged: the post-delete ratio of at least 1.35, the latency
   limits, the miss count of "big list", the digests and the data
   checks. "Active defrag pubsub" needs no change: its 69 MB of data
   keeps the floor under the 5% threshold.

2. "Active defrag can't be triggered during replicaof database flush".

   The sync flushes the replica within a few ms of REPLICAOF, before the
   next cron tick, so defrag starts on what the flush leaves. jemalloc
   leaves about 22 MB of fragmentation there. challoc's cache GC returns
   the freed slots (an epoch write runs the main thread's GC), which
   leaves 1.6 to 3.4 MB, so at the stock 2mb defrag sometimes never
   starts ("defrag not started."). Under challoc the test starts at 1mb
   and goes back to 2mb once defrag has started. Staying at 1mb would
   keep defrag running on the 1.6..1.8 MB floor of item 1.

Not changed: "main dictionary" and "big keys". Without challoc's PR
#576, cached slots pin slabs until the 10 s periodic cache GC and "main
dictionary" fails. "big keys" fails now and then on its 30 ms
defrag-cycle latency limit: challoc measures 14 to 33 ms where jemalloc
reports no event over 5 ms. Those are challoc's to fix, not the test's.
---
 tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl | 64 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 49 insertions(+), 15 deletions(-)

diff --git a/tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl b/tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl
--- a/tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl
+++ b/tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl
@@ -109,6 +109,31 @@ run_solo {defrag} {
         }
     }
 
+    # challoc (MALLOC=challoc) puts the size classes up to 4 KiB in 64 KiB
+    # slabs; the bundled jemalloc's bin slabs are one to a few pages. Active
+    # defrag leaves a floor of partly used slabs that no pass recovers:
+    # about one slab per class, plus every slab that still holds a
+    # long-lived object the defragmenter does not move. Flushing every
+    # thread's cache (under gdb) changes it by less than 0.2 MB, so it is
+    # not cache residue. Measured at the end of these tests (x86_64,
+    # quantum 8): 1.5 to 1.9 MB when each test runs on a fresh server,
+    # 3.0 to 4.8 MB in this file's sequence (objects left by the earlier
+    # tests pin more slabs), against 0.5 to 2.5 MB under jemalloc. These
+    # tests end with 25 to 30 MB of data outside the Lua arena, so the floor
+    # alone is a ratio of 1.06 to 1.19. That is above their 1.05..1.1
+    # targets, and the floor is above their 100kb..2mb
+    # active-defrag-ignore-bytes, so the defragmenter never stops: it
+    # rescans the keyspace without moving anything (hundreds of millions of
+    # misses in the 50 s wait). Under challoc they stop at 6mb and accept a
+    # ratio of 1.25. The deletes still leave 1.8 to 2.1 (20 to 27 MB of
+    # fragmentation), which defrag has to bring down to the floor.
+    proc challoc_or {challoc_value value} {
+        if {[string match {*challoc*} [s mem_allocator]]} {
+            return $challoc_value
+        }
+        return $value
+    }
+
     proc test_active_defrag {type} {
 
     # note: Disabling lookahead because it changes the number and order of allocations which interferes with defrag and causes tests to fail
@@ -260,7 +285,7 @@ run_solo {defrag} {
             r config set active-defrag-threshold-lower 5
             r config set active-defrag-cycle-min 65
             r config set active-defrag-cycle-max 75
-            r config set active-defrag-ignore-bytes 1500kb
+            r config set active-defrag-ignore-bytes [challoc_or 6mb 1500kb] ;# see challoc_or
             r config set maxmemory 0
 
             set n 50000
@@ -339,7 +364,7 @@ run_solo {defrag} {
                 }
 
                 # wait for the active defrag to stop working
-                wait_for_defrag_stop 500 100 1.05
+                wait_for_defrag_stop 500 100 [challoc_or 1.25 1.05] ;# challoc: 1.06..1.14
 
                 # test the fragmentation is lower
                 after 120 ;# serverCron only updates the info once in 100ms
@@ -622,7 +647,7 @@ run_solo {defrag} {
             r config set active-defrag-threshold-lower 5
             r config set active-defrag-cycle-min 65
             r config set active-defrag-cycle-max 75
-            r config set active-defrag-ignore-bytes 1500kb
+            r config set active-defrag-ignore-bytes [challoc_or 6mb 1500kb] ;# see challoc_or
             r config set maxmemory 0
 
             set n 50000
@@ -662,7 +687,7 @@ run_solo {defrag} {
                 puts "frag [s allocator_frag_ratio]"
                 puts "frag_bytes [s allocator_frag_bytes]"
             }
-            assert_lessthan [s allocator_frag_ratio] 1.05
+            assert_lessthan [s allocator_frag_ratio] [challoc_or 1.1 1.05] ;# challoc: 1.03..1.05
 
             # Verify IDMP structures were created
             set idmp_info [r xinfo stream idmpstream full]
@@ -703,7 +728,7 @@ run_solo {defrag} {
                 }
 
                 # wait for the active defrag to stop working
-                wait_for_defrag_stop 500 100 1.1
+                wait_for_defrag_stop 500 100 [challoc_or 1.25 1.1] ;# challoc: 1.07..1.16
 
                 # test the fragmentation is lower
                 after 120 ;# serverCron only updates the info once in 100ms
@@ -739,7 +764,7 @@ run_solo {defrag} {
             r config set active-defrag-threshold-lower 7
             r config set active-defrag-cycle-min 65
             r config set active-defrag-cycle-max 75
-            r config set active-defrag-ignore-bytes 1000kb
+            r config set active-defrag-ignore-bytes [challoc_or 6mb 1000kb] ;# see challoc_or
             r config set maxmemory 0
             r config set hash-max-listpack-value 512
             r config set hash-max-listpack-entries 10
@@ -778,7 +803,7 @@ run_solo {defrag} {
                 puts "frag [s allocator_frag_ratio]"
                 puts "frag_bytes [s allocator_frag_bytes]"
             }
-            assert_lessthan [s allocator_frag_ratio] 1.07
+            assert_lessthan [s allocator_frag_ratio] [challoc_or 1.1 1.07] ;# challoc: 1.03..1.08
 
             # Delete all the keys to create fragmentation
             for {set i 0} {$i < $n} {incr i} {
@@ -809,7 +834,7 @@ run_solo {defrag} {
                 }
 
                 # wait for the active defrag to stop working
-                wait_for_defrag_stop 500 100 1.07
+                wait_for_defrag_stop 500 100 [challoc_or 1.25 1.07] ;# challoc: 1.06..1.19
 
                 # test the fragmentation is lower
                 after 120 ;# serverCron only updates the info once in 100ms
@@ -837,7 +862,7 @@ run_solo {defrag} {
             }
             r config set active-defrag-cycle-min 65
             r config set active-defrag-cycle-max 75
-            r config set active-defrag-ignore-bytes 1000kb
+            r config set active-defrag-ignore-bytes [challoc_or 6mb 1000kb] ;# see challoc_or
             r config set maxmemory 0
 
             # Create some clients so that they are distributed among different io threads.
@@ -882,7 +907,7 @@ run_solo {defrag} {
                 puts "frag [s allocator_frag_ratio]"
                 puts "frag_bytes [s allocator_frag_bytes]"
             }
-            assert_lessthan [s allocator_frag_ratio] 1.05
+            assert_lessthan [s allocator_frag_ratio] [challoc_or 1.1 1.05] ;# challoc: 1.01..1.05
 
             # Delete keys with even indices to create fragmentation.
             for {set i 0} {$i < [llength $clients]} {incr i} {
@@ -923,11 +948,11 @@ run_solo {defrag} {
 
                 # wait for the active defrag to stop working
                 if {$io_threads == 1} {
-                    wait_for_defrag_stop 500 100 1.05
+                    wait_for_defrag_stop 500 100 [challoc_or 1.25 1.05] ;# challoc: 1.08..1.16
                 } else {
                     # TODO: When multithreading is enabled, argv may be created in the io thread
                     # and kept in the main thread, which can cause fragmentation to become worse.
-                    wait_for_defrag_stop 500 100 1.1
+                    wait_for_defrag_stop 500 100 [challoc_or 1.25 1.1]
                 }
 
                 # test the fragmentation is lower
@@ -952,7 +977,7 @@ run_solo {defrag} {
             r config set active-defrag-threshold-lower 5
             r config set active-defrag-cycle-min 65
             r config set active-defrag-cycle-max 75
-            r config set active-defrag-ignore-bytes 2mb
+            r config set active-defrag-ignore-bytes [challoc_or 6mb 2mb] ;# see challoc_or
             r config set maxmemory 0
             r config set list-max-ziplist-size 1 ;# list of 100k items will have 100k quicklist nodes
 
@@ -1274,7 +1299,7 @@ run_solo {defrag} {
             r config set active-defrag-threshold-lower 5
             r config set active-defrag-cycle-min 65
             r config set active-defrag-cycle-max 75
-            r config set active-defrag-ignore-bytes 100kb
+            r config set active-defrag-ignore-bytes [challoc_or 6mb 100kb] ;# see challoc_or
             r config set maxmemory 0
             # Field count drives the encoding: the 3-field template stays
             # template-listpack, the 40-field template becomes template-array.
@@ -1429,7 +1454,15 @@ run_solo {defrag} {
                 $replica config set active-defrag-threshold-lower 5
                 $replica config set active-defrag-cycle-min 65
                 $replica config set active-defrag-cycle-max 75
-                $replica config set active-defrag-ignore-bytes 2mb
+                # challoc: defrag starts only after the flush (the sync's
+                # flush runs within a few ms of REPLICAOF, before the next
+                # cron), on what the flush leaves. The bundled jemalloc
+                # leaves about 22 MB of fragmentation there; challoc's cache
+                # GC returns the freed slots, leaving 1.6 to 3.4 MB, and at
+                # 2mb defrag sometimes never starts. Start at 1mb; restored
+                # to 2mb once it runs, since the 1.6..1.8 MB floor
+                # (challoc_or) would otherwise keep it running.
+                $replica config set active-defrag-ignore-bytes [challoc_or 1mb 2mb]
 
                 # add a mass of string keys
                 set count 0
@@ -1468,6 +1501,7 @@ run_solo {defrag} {
                         puts [$replica memory malloc-stats]
                         fail "defrag not started."
                     }
+                    $replica config set active-defrag-ignore-bytes 2mb
 
                     wait_for_sync $replica
 
